NETWORK TRANSPORT

Contact/lobby and gameplay connections use UDP by default and can be selected
independently.  To use TCP for both, start both endpoints with matching
options, for example:

        xpilot-ng-server -contactTransport tcp -gameTransport tcp [options]
        xpilot-ng-sdl -contactTransport tcp -gameTransport tcp \
            -join server.example

For direct connections, the client and server must select the same value
(`udp` or `tcp`) for each transport; there is no automatic fallback.  The
`-port` option identifies the numeric contact port using the selected contact
transport.  TCP contact connections require an explicit server address or a
metaserver entry because LAN broadcast discovery is available only with UDP.

Metaserver advertisements include both transport selections.  The SDL and X11
clients apply the advertised values automatically when a server is selected;
legacy advertisements without transport metadata are treated as UDP contact
and UDP gameplay servers.  Metaserver query and reporting traffic continues to
use its existing protocols.

The server's `clientPortStart` and `clientPortEnd` range applies to the
selected gameplay protocol.  On clients it also applies to gameplay and the
traditional UDP contact socket; TCP contact connections use an ephemeral
local port.

TCP gameplay records contain a two-byte network-order payload length followed
by an unchanged XPilot packet payload.  Server recordings must be replayed
with the same `gameTransport` value that was used while recording.



